{"gid":"2026_02_GB_NYJ","week":2,"away":"GB","home":"NYJ","away_col":"#FFD84D","home_col":"#2FC98A","spread":-6.5,"total":42.5,"fav":"GB","hpts":18,"apts":24,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 20 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> GB are run-first (36% pass when leading) into a NYJ front that bleeds <strong>132 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.62 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>NYJ</strong> welcome the <strong>GB</strong> as underdogs of <strong>6.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>42.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>GB 24\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>40% of its snaps with GB in front</strong>, <strong>36% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with GB chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>GB should control</strong>. A favorite of 6 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>GB</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>NYJ</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>NYJ</strong> to run more total plays than GB \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for NYJ's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Breece Hall</strong> \u2014 GB are stingy against the run (110 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 GB hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Breece Hall</strong> is a bell-cow (66% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.50 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p>In the red zone NYJ throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to GB \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>GB \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>GB are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>36%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 6-point favorite projected to lead for 40% of snaps, <strong>GB</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>12 carries for 52 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as GB salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Christian Watson</strong> (5 targets, 52 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when GB lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 51 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYJ \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYJ are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NYJ project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Breece Hall</strong>'s rushing projection (14 carries, 63 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NYJ funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 54 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mason Taylor</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 34 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"GB","col":"#FFD84D","players":[{"name":"Jordan Love","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pa